#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124310 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "0fc616fa-c901-53ff-acab-cdada6bb5ce2" :question/math-academy-id "e-80" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^4$. Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{3x^{4} + 9x}{6x^{4} - x^{3}} &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{3x^{4}}{x^{4}} + \\frac{9x}{x^{4}})}{(\\frac{6x^{4}}{x^{4}} - \\frac{x^{3}}{x^{4}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(3 + \\frac{9}{x^{3}})}{(6 - \\frac{1}{x})} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 + 0}{6 - 0} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124312 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "b648ed80-e2e1-57c4-bd37-147d92315e90" :question/math-academy-id "e-81" :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^2$. Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{4 - 5x - 2x^{3}}{x^{2} + 3} &= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{-2x^{3}}{x^{2}} - \\frac{5x}{x^{2}} + \\frac{4}{x^{2}})}{(\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2}} + \\frac{3}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}\\frac{(-2x - \\frac{5}{x} + \\frac{4}{x^{2}})}{(1 + \\frac{3}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}(\\frac{-2x + 0 + 0}{1 + 0}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to - \\infty}(-2x) \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124313 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c3798fe4-058f-5970-a903-65c654abb788" :question/math-academy-id "e-82" :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ with $f(x)=\\dfrac{2x^3+1}{x^3-15x}$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{2x^{3} + 1}{x^{3} - 15x} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{2x^{3}}{x^{3}} + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})}{(\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{15x}{x^{3}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(2 + \\frac{1}{x^{3}})}{(1 - \\frac{15}{x^{2}})} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 + 0}{1 - 0} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124326 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/id #uuid "32a8e4bb-4191-55a8-883b-baa36f67b061" :question/math-academy-id "e-83" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^5-10}{x^3-8x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is\n\n$$\ny=\\lim_{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty}f(x) &= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{x^{5} - 10}{x^{3} - 8x^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(\\frac{x^{5}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}})}{(\\frac{x^{3}}{x^{3}} - \\frac{8x^{2}}{x^{3}})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}\\frac{(x^{2} - \\frac{10}{x^{3}})}{(1 - \\frac{8}{x})} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}(\\frac{x^{2} - 0}{1 - 0}) \\\\\n&= \\lim_{x \\to \\infty}x^{2} \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ed9048f-464b-5582-9fb3-749f129d40ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275143} {:answer/id #uuid "02a2fc35-a4b0-588f-8798-8c9b91ef99f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275144} {:answer/id #uuid "2e8dbaa4-8746-5d9a-bc39-446be8155800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275145} {:answer/id #uuid "274ae366-af77-5997-a9cd-880ae0063035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275146} {:answer/id #uuid "8d36dfc6-ccb1-53de-a29e-34e5f53fc370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "274ae366-af77-5997-a9cd-880ae0063035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275146} :answer-field/id #uuid "96a5b01e-0952-5823-aec0-33b8b2827a27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275142}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0017da64-a97d-5a88-b013-5a76140818dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-101321" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}+x-2}{6x-5}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{4}+x-2}{6x-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{4}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{6x}{x}-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8{x}^{3}+1-\\frac{2}{x})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8{x}^{3}+1-0)}{(6-0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5ae67f-b653-5ce6-b755-55e2b4ac69fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275150} {:answer/id #uuid "758c60e0-484f-51dc-88c5-d4592084468d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275151} {:answer/id #uuid "5b309239-33c9-5469-b6c3-c6f7fccdda0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275152} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8b9d58-831e-58f0-a430-a84e0eb85540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275153} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0ad02a-deaf-544e-9ef5-e0a1ac6da894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "758c60e0-484f-51dc-88c5-d4592084468d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275151} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc4faa7a-ce38-5c4e-adb1-74384ffbfacf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275149}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa21a5eb-170d-5bd2-91bb-346cb4d6766e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106694" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{2x-{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}+5}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{2x-{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-{x}^{2})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{0-{x}^{2}}{1-0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d289c4d-66f9-5bc3-ba56-6f40a3dd4628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275157} {:answer/id #uuid "57140c10-c243-59a6-a36d-d4ee1187cf49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275158} {:answer/id #uuid "eec3b875-66a9-5dff-878d-62bcc4f5aad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275159} {:answer/id #uuid "938099ab-6674-5275-bf78-41bf3fd9fe1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275160} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd6c2f5-fa61-51d4-8143-2a3ff0cb7e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec3b875-66a9-5dff-878d-62bcc4f5aad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275159} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf9256b-03e8-5ac3-88fd-10615dd8ed9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275156}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "062f25d0-e65b-58a6-96b7-d2cc83f47308" :question/math-academy-id "q-106702" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{5}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11{x}^{5}-9{x}^{6}}{2{x}^{5}-1} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{11{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{9{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(11-9x)}{(2-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{11-9x}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(11-9x) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6f8e3ec-a75e-53fe-b29a-1366c55b7a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6f8e3ec-a75e-53fe-b29a-1366c55b7a1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275164} :answer-field/id #uuid "286f59c2-1895-5098-9962-872de30b9e95" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275163}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a921d240-c753-5086-9f3e-3453deca86b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-339421" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{6}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{4{x}^{6}-5x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{5x}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}})}{(4-\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{4-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b99e024-0827-5449-bdf4-75125c45a836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b99e024-0827-5449-bdf4-75125c45a836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275167} :answer-field/id #uuid "53891a93-adff-583b-ad1e-85c1380df81f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275166}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "000da502-b1b5-5116-a1b7-f93b9f904ead" :question/math-academy-id "q-339507" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For large negative values of $x,$ we have $|-12x+5|=-12x+5.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{|-12x+5|}{4x+3}=\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12x+5}{4x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-12x}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-12+\\frac{5}{x})}{(4+\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12+0}{4+0} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74885b07-6eb9-512f-88de-44f3f84c5a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74885b07-6eb9-512f-88de-44f3f84c5a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275170} :answer-field/id #uuid "59067eab-dbc9-5efb-a806-44544690b377" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275169}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c34786e-7329-5ee5-938e-61e317be395c" :question/math-academy-id "q-339612" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+x+5}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{6}+4x}{{x}^{2}+5} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{6}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(1+\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{4}+0)}{(1+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f968f92-d46b-5506-b425-6806802a0fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f968f92-d46b-5506-b425-6806802a0fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275173} :answer-field/id #uuid "d360b2b0-6746-58ae-a7bd-df140cd0dc4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275172}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3bcb15bd-9c0c-5c65-97b9-07fcb59a2733" :question/math-academy-id "q-339621" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{9}-6{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}+10x}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-{x}^{9}-6{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{3}+10x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-{x}^{9}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-{x}^{6}-\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\text{ }\\frac{-{x}^{6}-0+0}{1+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,-{x}^{6} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ba531cc-1057-551f-9a86-2fc85ecd71b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275176} {:answer/id #uuid "1336ebc1-00e8-5060-99c4-9d61efe3b6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba531cc-1057-551f-9a86-2fc85ecd71b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275176} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf039c6-d675-57a2-b9d8-6e8d13947e79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275175}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a616dfb2-527b-5098-b8ce-2796ed00b6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-339623" :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1}=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{4}-2}{5{x}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}{(5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{5-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08f099b6-c4d1-5d21-ac2a-c02d25a98fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275180} {:answer/id #uuid "97633eac-78cb-5bea-bd54-88180978b6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08f099b6-c4d1-5d21-ac2a-c02d25a98fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275180} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d88c5c2-353b-5e3c-9402-269cde16a082" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275179}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bc264da-3bc4-5561-9fa9-e0a37b51fa53" :question/math-academy-id "q-339629" :question/problem "The horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3}$ is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6{x}^{4}+6{x}^{2}-2}{2{x}^{4}+3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{6{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{6{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(6+\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}})}{(2+\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6+0+0}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91924e9c-268a-549b-acdb-3bcd1759bd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91924e9c-268a-549b-acdb-3bcd1759bd84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275184} :answer-field/id #uuid "94f42605-5fb1-5527-bedd-e379c95369fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275183}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6f0ac3c-98f9-5ddd-b191-1756531965cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-339641" :question/problem "What is the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ for $x>0$ with $f(x)=\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}?$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=$\n\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|3-8x|=8x-3.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|3-8x|}{4x+1}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1}.\n$$\n\nThe horizontal asymptote for $x>0$ is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8x-3}{4x+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8x}{x}-\\frac{3}{x})}{(\\frac{4x}{x}+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8-\\frac{3}{x})}{(4+\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf433b06-7dfb-52df-b2e4-e0ffaf91ca48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275187} {:answer/id #uuid "4073f650-a879-577e-a026-a773f80dda28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275188} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b5b82b-89ba-5989-bec7-2d9dcac1475b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275189} {:answer/id #uuid "0f94dc29-5f50-580c-9bdf-c64ab96fe471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275190} {:answer/id #uuid "ee46ed1b-b405-5a66-bc4a-3fe718d4890f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf433b06-7dfb-52df-b2e4-e0ffaf91ca48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275187} :answer-field/id #uuid "9702e721-edd5-5b4d-8aba-b5576eb32250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275186}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d425093e-9004-5661-b7d6-1c6b6cc8fb5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-35513" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{4}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b092275-0441-51f6-a010-037dfba6f9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121985} {:answer/id #uuid "97fada48-c296-548c-b940-338cfffbd117" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121986} {:answer/id #uuid "e2569ab0-02c7-5ac5-b846-3a3d0cca1aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121987} {:answer/id #uuid "01ab5ced-488e-53de-a635-3fb03079cdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121988} {:answer/id #uuid "7f242d0b-0fd1-5380-b194-6c29749e0d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01ab5ced-488e-53de-a635-3fb03079cdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121988} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b4b97e-25e9-5c1e-b9ae-450448f2ae46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121984}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24c1ddd3-5086-5a99-bc61-14c84e2545e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35763" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x) = \\frac{2x + 5}{2x^{3} + 2x + 6}$." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+5}{2{x}^{3}+2x+6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}})}{(2+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{2+0+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee15db10-b92c-5f48-8983-e602068c7e08" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Asymptote of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc2d79df-16bd-54f3-90e0-fae80e320a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275194} {:answer/id #uuid "c745edd4-05d9-53f7-abab-2c20c7db0f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275195} {:answer/id #uuid "852e1584-cf82-51dd-b11f-0e961c5afcb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275196} {:answer/id #uuid "c4084f72-1b9c-5365-8522-d0b138dc08fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275197} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3bc636-484d-5b85-96b3-0d5c42d00ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4084f72-1b9c-5365-8522-d0b138dc08fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275197} :answer-field/id #uuid "35ca9071-6662-5afe-ba8d-2d66e1d227d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275193}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89041eed-f9ba-5e78-85a0-a5586670c312" :question/math-academy-id "q-35831" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)=\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{6{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{14{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{14}{(6+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{6+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)$ is $y=\\frac{7}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "853a1a88-bb31-595b-a05d-faec55e0de38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122045} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc1e936-5e6f-579a-b63b-656301cb3577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122046} {:answer/id #uuid "9235c505-e485-526f-a286-a4e4a3a80ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122047} {:answer/id #uuid "11f32076-7fd0-5744-bc96-6b7297bc4538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122048} {:answer/id #uuid "702f8245-c1b6-5ca6-b6e6-543e0e863129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "702f8245-c1b6-5ca6-b6e6-543e0e863129" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122049} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30321be-736d-5b74-8428-7d00bbc108a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122044}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5a61e3c-7091-5d14-ae25-993daf76ef12" :question/math-academy-id "q-35834" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y = f(x)$ where $f(x) = \\frac{12x - 3x^{4}}{3x^{2} + 7}$." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{12x-3{x}^{4}}{3{x}^{2}+7} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12x}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{12}{x}-3{x}^{2})}{(3+\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{0-3{x}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "646cf329-dca1-5645-a017-462ba94808b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275201} {:answer/id #uuid "03f2e178-1832-5c45-8c83-767c50ff3816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275202} {:answer/id #uuid "9b96f6db-9254-5076-a152-328b8b181782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275203} {:answer/id #uuid "e668ad1c-25b1-5e4c-b042-4529e8390d74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Does not exist." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275204} {:answer/id #uuid "543b94e1-01fe-5629-8725-915cbdb02bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "646cf329-dca1-5645-a017-462ba94808b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275201} :answer-field/id #uuid "edf75a5a-69cd-5b59-a1a1-e773f892ab7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275200}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6dd55ea7-75b6-5df0-93a0-7e4cd72a8e5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-3762" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}}{6{x}^{3}-9} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(-2+\\frac{1}{x})}{(6-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+0}{6-0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48045986-d66a-5b6e-8fae-371fda1e15c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275208} {:answer/id #uuid "2074eaac-36ee-5b01-b963-65c00801fa7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275209} {:answer/id #uuid "d9462320-0b8b-53fa-87df-1049a8dbc7f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275210} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a8fda3-5b30-584d-a55d-a1da2f17b2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275211} {:answer/id #uuid "154be909-53cf-5bad-82f7-b8cbffd30843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48045986-d66a-5b6e-8fae-371fda1e15c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275208} :answer-field/id #uuid "30efd1b0-3f2a-5298-b6d5-c26d836788c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275207}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8e244de-d84d-53fe-b83d-97c217781212" :question/math-academy-id "q-48713" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|7x-4|}{2x-6}=$" :question/worked-solution "For large positive values of $x,$ we have $|7x-4|=7x-4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to \\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{|7x-4|}{2x-6}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{7x-4}{2x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{7x}{x}-\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{2x}{x}-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(7-\\frac{4}{x})}{(2-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32cbf366-20bd-5a52-a14f-83add698bf07" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Rational Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "431199d7-afd6-5755-93a5-8ca03719f3a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275215} {:answer/id #uuid "4d0df227-676e-5ddd-b686-3e46bd8d1c3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275216} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e869f3-e71f-5b7b-b265-fd904e1ac8b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275217} {:answer/id #uuid "439f1c9c-8d56-5ce8-94dd-6874dfe72c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275218} {:answer/id #uuid "a986ae56-c5b8-56fa-8af8-4edae2cd9475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "439f1c9c-8d56-5ce8-94dd-6874dfe72c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275218} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b3e18b-68f8-5506-a354-9c4838d1ebee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275214}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51738651-486e-595b-81e7-037fa3f30444" :question/math-academy-id "q-48925" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|6x-4|}{3x+4}=$" :question/worked-solution "For sufficiently large negative values of $x,$ we have $|6x-4|=-(6x-4)=-6x+4.$ Consequently, for $x\\to -\\infty ,$ we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|6x-4|}{3x+4}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{1}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-6x+4}{3x+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-6x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-6+\\frac{4}{x})}{(3+\\frac{4}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+0}{3+0} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "020c3de3-bcaf-5ade-ba50-2f15e344157d" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Limit at Infinity: Infinite Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb19bf2c-b246-58ae-9a01-dbc27873fffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275222} {:answer/id #uuid "639aecb5-788d-592b-9191-6c127d73fd3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275223} {:answer/id #uuid "a35826e3-9244-560e-bae1-16d0d8301bae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275224} {:answer/id #uuid "9d78b262-8211-5a33-bcbc-d2c3c6a652e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Does not exist." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275225} {:answer/id #uuid "c40cae5c-e380-5fc9-89d2-2763c1f99aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "639aecb5-788d-592b-9191-6c127d73fd3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275223} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8729dfa-1a89-52bd-bb2c-be06765f0c65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b5e5447-1086-5ca5-9f04-9cb0cddc3c7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-68042" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{3{x}^{4}+2}{{x}^{3}-7x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{7x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{7}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{(3x+0)}{(1-0+0)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\,\\,3x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9f8a9f0-2934-5bb2-acc1-705b289fcf96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275229} {:answer/id #uuid "aabbba00-c010-5552-8c1b-3bbd78777d5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275230} {:answer/id #uuid "a314f518-cb1f-515f-bc4c-dcc6af849905" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275231} {:answer/id #uuid "36b5fef7-f91a-53f1-be44-a8476d359d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275232} {:answer/id #uuid "d9df4097-1963-5b33-aadd-b4e266535f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36b5fef7-f91a-53f1-be44-a8476d359d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275232} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa861b8-d4c7-5b09-b7dd-c98a15b62ed1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275228}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10717fa0-edd5-574c-b927-2b9585138eba" :question/math-academy-id "q-68072" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{4}+3}{2{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-7{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-7{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(2-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-7{x}^{2}+0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bde258b7-1654-5e73-8703-0e42371db8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying When a Rational Function has No Horizontal Asymptote"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fd7aaa5-f065-54fe-aa19-b77d0d23be0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275236} {:answer/id #uuid "4e68efbf-9a54-5932-bd73-38c02dadc642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275237} {:answer/id #uuid "7179d099-ba6d-5ae7-a0d6-faac13f6c974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275238} {:answer/id #uuid "a1776e98-0fb2-566f-acff-6ef5d0217776" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275239} {:answer/id #uuid "d27ad860-6d8c-58ad-b434-e6d399764bd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7179d099-ba6d-5ae7-a0d6-faac13f6c974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptote" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275238} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeaf0918-d433-514c-9d30-7b9bfb23ef81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275235}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb18f206-4708-5566-8244-d67a975a9398" :question/math-academy-id "q-78013" :question/problem "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{4{x}^{2}+5}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{x}^{2}+5}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{5}{x})}{(\\frac{2x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+\\frac{5}{x})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(4x+0)}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2x \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."}]}
